course: dictionary
lesson: 5779
title: A word from far away
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A dictionary tells you what a word means, and the sentences around it show how it is used. Read both. «Picture a world music fair: a Ukrainian bandura with dozens of strings, a Chinese erhu with only two, an Indian sitar, a West African kora that is half harp, half lute.» The list of instruments is your clue: a lute is one of the instruments at the fair.
explain: The dictionary describes a lute: it has a pear-shaped body, a bent-back neck, and strings that you pluck with your fingers. Picture those features and you can see the instrument. A lute is a stringed instrument, so you would not pluck a drum, blow a trumpet or bang a tambourine.
explain: Some words travel between languages. The lute got its name from the Arabic word «oud», so its word has a long journey. In history, lutes were popular in Europe in the Middle Ages and the Renaissance, and minstrels and courtiers played them. If a story says a bard sings at the castle with a lute, you know it is an instrument.
explain: Lute can also mean a sticky paste used to seal joints, but this meaning is rare. When a word has two meanings, use the sentence to choose. «Play the lute» and «lute music» point clearly to the instrument.
fact: A lute is an old stringed instrument with a pear-shaped body and a bent-back neck.
fact: You play a lute by plucking the strings with your fingers.
fact: The lute was very popular in Europe in the Middle Ages and the Renaissance.
fact: The lute came from the Arabic word «oud».
fact: Lute can also mean a sticky paste used to seal joints, but this meaning is rare.
exercise: In «a West African kora that is half harp, half lute», what is a lute being compared with? | A drum | A harp | *Another stringed instrument | A kind of paste
exercise: Which option describes how a lute is played? | By hitting it with sticks | *By plucking the strings with the fingers | By blowing into it | By sliding a bow across it
exercise: Where does the name lute come from? | The Chinese word erhu | *The Arabic word «oud» | A Scottish word meaning grey and wet | A Latin word for bread
test: In «play the lute» and «lute music», what does lute mean? | A bard who sings at a castle | *A stringed musical instrument | A kind of harp with no strings | A sticky paste used to seal joints
test: Which statement is a fact from this lesson? | A lute is played by blowing into it | The lute is a West African drum | *The lute was very popular in Europe in the Middle Ages | A lute has a square body
exam: Read «A bard with a lute sings at the castle.» Why is lute the instrument here, not the paste? | Bards use paste to seal joints | *A bard sings and plays music, so the sentence needs an instrument | Lutes are only found in the Scottish Highlands | The castle has thick walls
